Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.50,0:00:11.80,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ 'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves ♪\N♪ Did gyre and gimble in the wabe; ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.30,0:00:23.10,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ All mimsy were the borogoves, ♪\N♪ And the mome raths outgrabe. ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.60,0:00:34.20,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ "Beware the Jabberwock, my son! ♪\N♪ The jaws that bite, the claws that catch! ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.90,0:00:45.40,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Beware the Jubjub bird, and shun ♪\N♪ The frumious Bandersnatch!" ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.90,0:00:56.50,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ He took his vorpal sword in hand: ♪\N♪ Long time the manxome foe he sought— ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.10,0:01:07.90,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ So rested he by the Tumtum tree, ♪\N♪ And stood awhile in thought. ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:01:08.40,0:01:19.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ And as in uffish thought he stood, ♪\N♪ The Jabberwock, with eyes of flame, ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.60,0:01:30.20,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Came whiffling through the tulgey wood, ♪\N♪ And burbled as it came! ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.90,0:01:41.50,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ One, two! One, two! And through and through ♪\N♪ The vorpal blade went snicker-snack! ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:01:42.10,0:01:58.50,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ He left it dead, and with its head ♪\N♪ He went galumphing back. ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.20,0:02:03.90,Default,,0,0,0,,(Alice) A mad tea-party...
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.50,0:02:08.20,Default,,0,0,0,,There was a table set out under a tree in front of the house.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.50,0:02:08.20,Default,,0,0,0,,There was a table set out under a tree in front of the house.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.80,0:02:12.40,Default,,0,0,0,,The March Hare and the Hatter were having tea at it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.00,0:02:16.70,Default,,0,0,0,,A Dormouse was sitting between them, fast asleep.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.20,0:02:21.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The other two were using it as a cushion, resting their elbows on it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.10,0:02:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,They were talking right over its head.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.40,0:02:28.80,Default,,0,0,0,,*Very uncomfortable for the Dormouse...*
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.30,0:02:32.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Only, as it's asleep, I suppose it doesn't mind.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:33.40,0:02:36.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The table was a large one, but the three were all crowded together at one corner of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.30,0:02:39.50,Default,,0,0,0,,No room! No room!
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.10,0:02:42.50,Default,,0,0,0,,There's plenty of room!
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.10,0:02:46.40,Default,,0,0,0,,She sat down in a large arm-chair at one end of the table.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.90,0:02:48.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Have some wine.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.50,0:02:51.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I don't see any wine.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:52.40,0:02:54.20,Default,,0,0,0,,There isn't any.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.80,0:02:57.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then it wasn't very civil of you to offer it.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:58.40,0:03:01.80,Default,,0,0,0,,It wasn't very civil of you to sit down without being invited.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:02.30,0:03:05.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I didn't know it was your table; it's laid for a great many more than three.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.50,0:03:08.70,Default,,0,0,0,,Your hair wants cutting.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.30,0:03:12.80,Default,,0,0,0,,You should learn not to make personal remarks; it's very rude.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.40,0:03:16.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Why is a raven like a writing-desk?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.80,0:03:19.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Come, we shall have some fun now!
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.10,0:03:22.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I'm glad they've begun asking riddles.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.40,0:03:25.80,Default,,0,0,0,,I believe I can guess that.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.40,0:03:29.70,Default,,0,0,0,,Do you mean that you think you can find out the answer to it?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.30,0:03:31.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Exactly so.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.50,0:03:34.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Then you should say what you mean.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.40,0:03:38.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I do; at least—at least I mean what I say—that's the same thing, you know.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:39.40,0:03:41.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Not the same thing a bit!
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.30,0:03:46.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You might just as well say that "I see what I eat" is the same thing as "I eat what I see"!
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.40,0:03:51.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You might just as well say that "I like what I get" is the same thing as "I get what I like"!
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.50,0:03:56.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You might just as well say that "I breathe when I sleep" is the same thing as "I sleep when I breathe"!
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.40,0:03:59.80,Default,,0,0,0,,It IS the same thing with you.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.40,0:04:04.50,Action_Parenthetical,,0,0,0,,(shuffling tea cups) The conversation here dropped, and the party sat silent for a minute.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:05.10,0:04:09.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice thought over all she could remember about ravens and writing-desks, which wasn't much.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.40,0:04:13.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Hatter was the first to break the silence.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.40,0:04:16.90,Default,,0,0,0,,What day of the month is it?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.50,0:04:21.50,Default,,0,0,0,,He had taken his watch out of his pocket, and was looking at it uneasily.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.10,0:04:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,He shook it every now and then, and put it to his ear.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.40,0:04:28.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The fourth.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.80,0:04:30.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Two days wrong!
Dialogue: 0,0:04:31.40,0:04:33.50,Default,,0,0,0,,(sighing) I told you butter wouldn't suit the works!
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.10,0:04:36.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It was the BEST butter.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.80,0:04:40.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Yes, but some crumbs must have got in as well.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.80,0:04:43.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You shouldn't have put it in with the bread-knife.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.40,0:04:47.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The March Hare took the watch and looked at it gloomily.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.50,0:04:51.90,Default,,0,0,0,,He dipped it into his cup of tea, and looked at it again.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.40,0:04:54.80,Default,,0,0,0,,But he could think of nothing better to say than his first remark.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.30,0:04:57.50,Default,,0,0,0,,It was the BEST butter, you know.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.10,0:05:01.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice had been looking over his shoulder with some curiosity.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:02.40,0:05:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,What a funny watch!
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.40,0:05:08.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It tells the day of the month, and doesn't tell what o'clock it is!
Dialogue: 0,0:05:09.50,0:05:11.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Why should it?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.10,0:05:14.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Does your watch tell you what year it is?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.30,0:05:16.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Of course not.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.50,0:05:20.90,Default,,0,0,0,,But that's because it stays the same year for such a long time together.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.50,0:05:23.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Which is just the case with mine.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.40,0:05:27.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice felt dreadfully puzzled.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.10,0:05:31.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Hatter's remark seemed to have no sort of meaning in it, and yet it was certainly English.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:32.40,0:05:34.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I don't quite understand you.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:35.40,0:05:37.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse is asleep again.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.40,0:05:41.20,Default,,0,0,0,,He poured a little hot tea upon its nose.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:41.80,0:05:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse shook its head impatiently, and said, without opening its eyes...
Dialogue: 0,0:05:46.40,0:05:50.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Of course, of course; just what I was going to remark myself.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:51.50,0:05:53.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Have you guessed the riddle yet?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.40,0:05:56.50,Default,,0,0,0,,No, I give it up.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.10,0:05:58.80,Default,,0,0,0,,What's the answer?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:59.40,0:06:01.50,Default,,0,0,0,,I haven't the slightest idea.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.10,0:06:03.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Nor I.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.40,0:06:08.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice sighed wearily.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.80,0:06:12.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I think you might do something better with the time than waste it in asking riddles that have no answers.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:13.40,0:06:17.90,Default,,0,0,0,,If you knew Time as well as I do, you wouldn't talk about wasting IT. It's HIM.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:18.40,0:06:20.50,Default,,0,0,0,,I don't know what you mean.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:21.10,0:06:24.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Of course you don't!
Dialogue: 0,0:06:24.80,0:06:27.50,Default,,0,0,0,,I dare say you never even spoke to Time!
Dialogue: 0,0:06:28.10,0:06:31.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Perhaps not, but I know I have to beat time when I learn music.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.40,0:06:34.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Ah! That accounts for it.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:34.80,0:06:36.50,Default,,0,0,0,,He won't stand beating.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.10,0:06:40.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Now, if you only kept on good terms with him, he'd do almost anything you liked with the clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:41.40,0:06:44.90,Default,,0,0,0,,For instance, suppose it were nine o'clock in the morning, just time to begin lessons...
Dialogue: 0,0:06:45.40,0:06:48.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You'd only have to whisper a hint to Time, and round goes the clock in a twinkling!
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.50,0:06:51.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Half-past one, time for dinner!
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.10,0:06:54.50,Default,,0,0,0,,(whispering) I only wish it was.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.10,0:06:58.50,Default,,0,0,0,,That would be grand, certainly, but then—I shouldn't be hungry for it, you know.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:59.10,0:07:02.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Not at first, perhaps, but you could keep it to half-past one as long as you liked.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.10,0:07:05.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Is that the way you manage?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.80,0:07:09.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The Hatter shook his head mournfully.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.80,0:07:13.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Not I! We quarrelled last March—just before HE went mad, you know—
Dialogue: 0,0:07:14.40,0:07:16.90,Default,,0,0,0,,(pointing with his teaspoon at the March Hare)
Dialogue: 0,0:07:17.50,0:07:20.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It was at the great concert given by the Queen of Hearts.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:21.50,0:07:24.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And I had to sing...
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.80,0:07:28.40,Default,,0,0,0,,"Twinkle, twinkle, little bat! How I wonder what you're at!"
Dialogue: 0,0:07:29.00,0:07:31.40,Default,,0,0,0,,You know the song, perhaps?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.90,0:07:33.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I've heard something like it.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:34.50,0:07:37.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It goes on, you know, in this way...
Dialogue: 0,0:07:37.80,0:07:41.20,Default,,0,0,0,,"Up above the world you fly, like a tea-tray in the sky."
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.80,0:07:43.40,Default,,0,0,0,,"Twinkle, twinkle—"
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.00,0:07:47.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Here the Dormouse shook itself, and began singing in its sleep...
Dialogue: 0,0:07:48.10,0:07:54.90,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Twinkle, twinkle, twinkle, twinkle... ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:07:55.40,0:07:59.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And went on so long that they had to pinch it to make it stop.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.80,0:08:03.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Well, I'd hardly finished the first verse, when the Queen bawled out...
Dialogue: 0,0:08:04.00,0:08:06.50,Default,,0,0,0,,"He's murdering the time! Off with his head!"
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.10,0:08:08.90,Default,,0,0,0,,How dreadfully savage!
Dialogue: 0,0:08:09.50,0:08:12.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And ever since then, he won't do a thing I ask!
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.40,0:08:15.50,Default,,0,0,0,,It's always six o'clock now.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.10,0:08:18.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Is that the reason so many tea-things are put out here?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:19.10,0:08:22.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Yes, that's it. It's always tea-time.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:23.40,0:08:26.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And we've no time to wash the things between whiles.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.50,0:08:29.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then you keep moving round, I suppose?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:30.40,0:08:31.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Exactly so, as the things get used up.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.50,0:08:35.40,Default,,0,0,0,,But what happens when you come to the beginning again?
Dialogue: 0,0:08:36.00,0:08:39.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Suppose we change the subject. I'm getting tired of this.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:39.90,0:08:42.50,Default,,0,0,0,,I vote the young lady tells us a story.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:43.10,0:08:45.40,Default,,0,0,0,,I'm afraid I don't know one.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:46.00,0:08:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Then the Dormouse shall! Wake up, Dormouse!
Dialogue: 0,0:08:50.10,0:08:52.90,Default,,0,0,0,,They pinched it on both sides at once.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:53.40,0:08:55.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I wasn't asleep.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.50,0:08:59.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I heard every word you fellows were saying.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:00.50,0:09:02.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Tell us a story!
Dialogue: 0,0:09:02.80,0:09:04.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Yes, please do!
Dialogue: 0,0:09:05.10,0:09:07.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And be quick about it, or you'll be asleep again before it's done.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.10,0:09:11.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Once upon a time there were three little sisters...
Dialogue: 0,0:09:12.00,0:09:14.40,Default,,0,0,0,,And their names were Elsie, Lacie, and Tillie...
Dialogue: 0,0:09:15.00,0:09:17.80,Default,,0,0,0,,And they lived at the bottom of a well—
Dialogue: 0,0:09:18.40,0:09:20.50,Default,,0,0,0,,What did they live on?
Dialogue: 0,0:09:21.10,0:09:24.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice always took a great interest in questions of eating and drinking.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:24.80,0:09:26.90,Default,,0,0,0,,They lived on treacle.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:27.50,0:09:29.90,Default,,0,0,0,,They couldn't have done that, you know. It would have made them ill.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:30.40,0:09:32.20,Default,,0,0,0,,So they were. Very ill.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:32.80,0:09:35.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice tried to fancy to herself what such an extraordinary way of living would be like...
Dialogue: 0,0:09:36.40,0:09:38.50,Default,,0,0,0,,But it puzzled her too much.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:39.10,0:09:41.90,Default,,0,0,0,,But why did they live at the bottom of a well?
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.40,0:09:44.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Take some more tea.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:45.40,0:09:47.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I've had nothing yet, so I can't take more.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:48.40,0:09:51.50,Default,,0,0,0,,You mean you can't take LESS.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:52.10,0:09:55.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It's very easy to take MORE than nothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:55.80,0:09:58.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Nobody asked YOUR opinion.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:58.80,0:10:00.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Who's making personal remarks now?
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.20,0:10:03.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Hatter hungrily eyed the remaining cake.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.40,0:10:06.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice did not quite know what to say to this.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:07.50,0:10:10.20,Default,,0,0,0,,So she helped herself to some tea and bread-and-butter.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:10.80,0:10:13.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then she turned to the Dormouse, and repeated her question.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:14.50,0:10:17.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Why did they live at the bottom of a well?
Dialogue: 0,0:10:17.80,0:10:20.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse again took a minute or two to think about it.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:21.50,0:10:23.80,Default,,0,0,0,,It was a treacle-well.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:24.40,0:10:26.50,Default,,0,0,0,,There's no such thing!
Dialogue: 0,0:10:27.10,0:10:29.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Hatter and the March Hare went "Shsh! shsh!"
Dialogue: 0,0:10:30.50,0:10:33.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse sulkily remarked...
Dialogue: 0,0:10:34.40,0:10:37.50,Default,,0,0,0,,If you can't be civil, you'd better finish the story for yourself.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:38.10,0:10:40.20,Default,,0,0,0,,No, please go on!
Dialogue: 0,0:10:40.80,0:10:42.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I won't interrupt you any more.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:43.50,0:10:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,There may be ONE treacle-well.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:46.40,0:10:48.20,Default,,0,0,0,,One, indeed!
Dialogue: 0,0:10:48.80,0:10:51.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And so they were learning to draw...
Dialogue: 0,0:10:52.10,0:10:55.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse went on, yawning and rubbing its eyes.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:55.80,0:10:57.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It was getting very sleepy.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:58.40,0:11:01.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And they drew all manner of things...
Dialogue: 0,0:11:01.80,0:11:04.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Everything that begins with an M.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:05.10,0:11:06.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Why with an M?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:07.40,0:11:09.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Why not?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:09.80,0:11:11.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice was silent.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:12.10,0:11:15.40,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse had closed its eyes by this time.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:16.00,0:11:18.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It was fast asleep once more.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:19.50,0:11:22.80,Default,,0,0,0,,But on being pinched by the Hatter, it woke up with a little shriek.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:23.40,0:11:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,...that begins with an M, such as mouse-traps...
Dialogue: 0,0:11:26.50,0:11:29.40,Default,,0,0,0,,And the moon, and memory, and muchness...
Dialogue: 0,0:11:30.00,0:11:33.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You know you say things are "much of a muchness"—
Dialogue: 0,0:11:34.40,0:11:37.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Did you ever see such a thing as a drawing of a muchness?
Dialogue: 0,0:11:38.40,0:11:41.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Really, now you ask me, I don't think—
Dialogue: 0,0:11:41.80,0:11:43.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then you shouldn't talk.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:44.50,0:11:47.20,Default,,0,0,0,,This piece of rudeness was more than Alice could bear.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:47.80,0:11:51.20,Default,,0,0,0,,She got up in great disgust, and walked off.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:51.80,0:11:54.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Dormouse fell asleep instantly.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:55.50,0:11:59.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Neither of the others took the least notice of her going.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.80,0:12:03.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Though she looked back once or twice, half hoping that they would call after her.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:04.00,0:12:07.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The last time she saw them, they were trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:08.10,0:12:10.80,Default,,0,0,0,,At any rate I'll never go THERE again!
Dialogue: 0,0:12:11.40,0:12:14.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It's the stupidest tea-party I ever was at in all my life!
Dialogue: 0,0:12:14.80,0:12:17.90,Default,,0,0,0,,As she said this, she noticed a tree with a door leading right into it.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:18.40,0:12:20.50,Default,,0,0,0,,That's very curious!
Dialogue: 0,0:12:21.10,0:12:23.50,Default,,0,0,0,,But everything's curious today.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:24.10,0:12:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I think I may as well go in at once.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:26.50,0:12:28.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And in she went.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:28.80,0:12:31.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Once more she found herself in the long hall.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:32.40,0:12:35.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And close to the little glass table.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:35.80,0:12:38.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Now, I'll manage better this time.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:39.50,0:12:42.80,Default,,0,0,0,,She began by taking the little golden key, and unlocking the door that led into the garden.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:43.40,0:12:46.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then she set to work nibbling at the mushroom she kept in her pocket.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:47.40,0:12:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Until she was about a foot high.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:50.10,0:12:53.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Then she walked down the little passage.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:54.00,0:12:57.80,Default,,0,0,0,,And THEN she found herself at last in the beautiful garden.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:58.40,0:13:01.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Among the bright flower-beds and the cool fountains.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:01.80,0:13:05.20,Default,,0,0,0,,A large rose-tree stood near the entrance of the garden.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:05.80,0:13:08.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The roses growing on it were white, but there were three gardeners at it.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:09.50,0:13:12.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Busily painting them red.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:13.00,0:13:15.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice thought this a very curious thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:16.10,0:13:18.90,Default,,0,0,0,,She went nearer to watch them.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:19.50,0:13:22.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Just as she came up to them she heard one of them say...
Dialogue: 0,0:13:23.40,0:13:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Look out now, Five! Don't go splashing paint over me like that!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:26.50,0:13:29.20,Default,,0,0,0,,I couldn't help it. Seven flung my elbow.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:29.80,0:13:32.90,Default,,0,0,0,,That's right, Seven! Always lay the blame on others!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:33.40,0:13:35.50,Default,,0,0,0,,YOU'D better not talk!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:36.10,0:13:39.40,Default,,0,0,0,,I heard the Queen say only yesterday you deserved to be beheaded!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:40.00,0:13:41.80,Default,,0,0,0,,What for?
Dialogue: 0,0:13:42.40,0:13:44.50,Default,,0,0,0,,That's none of YOUR business, Two!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:45.10,0:13:47.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Yes, it IS his business! And I'll tell him!
Dialogue: 0,0:13:48.50,0:13:51.40,Default,,0,0,0,,It was for bringing the cook tulip-roots instead of onions.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:52.00,0:13:54.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Seven flung his brush down.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:00.10,0:14:02.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Well, of all the unjust things—
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.50,0:14:06.80,Default,,0,0,0,,He began, but his eye chanced to fall upon Alice, as she stood watching them.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:07.40,0:14:09.90,Default,,0,0,0,,He checked himself suddenly.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:10.50,0:14:12.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The others looked round also.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:13.40,0:14:15.50,Default,,0,0,0,,They all bowed low.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:16.10,0:14:18.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Would you tell me, please, why you are painting those roses?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:19.40,0:14:21.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Two began, in a low voice.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:22.10,0:14:24.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Why, the fact is, you see, Miss...
Dialogue: 0,0:14:25.50,0:14:28.80,Default,,0,0,0,,This here rose-tree ought to have been a RED rose-tree.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:29.40,0:14:32.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And we put a white one in by mistake.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:33.40,0:14:36.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off, you know.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:37.50,0:14:40.20,Default,,0,0,0,,So you see, Miss, we're doing our best, afore she comes, to—
Dialogue: 0,0:14:40.80,0:14:43.40,Default,,0,0,0,,At this moment Five, who had been anxiously looking across the garden, called out...
Dialogue: 0,0:14:43.90,0:14:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen! The Queen!
Dialogue: 0,0:14:46.50,0:14:49.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The three gardeners instantly threw themselves flat upon their faces.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:50.40,0:14:53.50,Action_Parenthetical,,0,0,0,,There was a sound of many footsteps.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:54.10,0:14:56.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice looked round, eager to see the Queen.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:57.50,0:15:00.80,Default,,0,0,0,,First came ten soldiers carrying clubs.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:01.40,0:15:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,These were all shaped like the three gardeners, oblong and flat.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:05.40,0:15:08.20,Default,,0,0,0,,With their hands and feet at the corners.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:08.80,0:15:11.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Next the ten courtiers.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:12.10,0:15:15.50,Default,,0,0,0,,These were ornamented all over with diamonds.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:16.10,0:15:18.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And walked two and two, as the soldiers did.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:19.50,0:15:22.80,Default,,0,0,0,,After these came the royal children.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:23.40,0:15:26.90,Default,,0,0,0,,There were ten of them, and the little dears came jumping merrily along hand in hand.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:27.40,0:15:29.20,Default,,0,0,0,,They were ornamented with hearts.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:29.80,0:15:32.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Next came the guests, mostly Kings and Queens.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:33.40,0:15:36.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And among them the White Rabbit.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:37.10,0:15:40.20,Default,,0,0,0,,He was talking in a hurried nervous manner.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:40.80,0:15:43.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Smiling at everything that was said.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:44.10,0:15:46.90,Default,,0,0,0,,He went by without noticing her.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:47.50,0:15:50.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Then followed the Knave of Hearts, bearing the King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:51.00,0:15:55.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And, last of all this grand procession, came THE KING AND QUEEN OF HEARTS.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:55.80,0:15:58.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice was rather doubtful whether she ought not to lie down on her face like the three gardeners.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:59.40,0:16:01.80,Default,,0,0,0,,But she could not remember ever having heard of such a rule.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:02.30,0:16:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And besides, what would be the use of a procession...
Dialogue: 0,0:16:05.50,0:16:08.20,Default,,0,0,0,,If people all had to lie down upon their faces, so that they couldn't see it?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:08.80,0:16:11.50,Default,,0,0,0,,So she stood still where she was, and waited.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:12.10,0:16:15.20,Default,,0,0,0,,When the procession came opposite to Alice, they all stopped and looked at her.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:15.80,0:16:17.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen said severely...
Dialogue: 0,0:16:18.40,0:16:20.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Who is this?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:20.80,0:16:23.50,Default,,0,0,0,,She said it to the Knave of Hearts, who only bowed and smiled in reply.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:24.10,0:16:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Idiot!
Dialogue: 0,0:16:26.50,0:16:28.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Tossing her head impatiently, she turned to Alice.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:29.50,0:16:31.50,Default,,0,0,0,,What is your name, child?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:32.10,0:16:34.20,Default,,0,0,0,,My name is Alice, so please your Majesty.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:34.80,0:16:37.90,Default,,0,0,0,,She said it very civilly, but she added to herself...
Dialogue: 0,0:16:38.40,0:16:41.20,Default,,0,0,0,,*Why, they're only a pack of cards, after all. I needn't be afraid of them!*
Dialogue: 0,0:16:41.80,0:16:44.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And who are THESE?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:44.80,0:16:47.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Pointing to the three gardeners who were lying round the rose-tree.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:48.40,0:16:51.80,Default,,0,0,0,,For, you see, as they were lying on their faces, and the pattern on their backs was the same...
Dialogue: 0,0:16:52.40,0:16:55.20,Default,,0,0,0,,She could not tell whether they were gardeners, or soldiers, or courtiers.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:55.80,0:16:57.90,Default,,0,0,0,,How should I know?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:58.40,0:17:00.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It's no business of MINE.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:01.50,0:17:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen turned crimson with fury, and, after glaring at her for a moment like a wild beast, screamed...
Dialogue: 0,0:17:05.40,0:17:07.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Off with her head! Off—
Dialogue: 0,0:17:08.10,0:17:09.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Nonsense!
Dialogue: 0,0:17:10.40,0:17:12.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice said very loudly and decidedly, and the Queen was silent.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:13.40,0:17:16.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The King laid his hand upon her arm, and timidly said...
Dialogue: 0,0:17:16.80,0:17:19.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Consider, my dear: she is only a child!
Dialogue: 0,0:17:20.40,0:17:23.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen turned angrily away from him.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:23.80,0:17:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Turn them over!
Dialogue: 0,0:17:24.40,0:17:28.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The Knave did so, with one foot.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:29.40,0:17:31.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Get up!
Dialogue: 0,0:17:32.10,0:17:34.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen cried in a shrill voice.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:34.80,0:17:38.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The three gardeners instantly jumped up, and began bowing to the King, the Queen, and everybody else.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:38.80,0:17:41.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Leave off that! You make me giddy.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:41.80,0:17:43.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Then, turning to the rose-tree, she went on...
Dialogue: 0,0:17:44.40,0:17:46.20,Default,,0,0,0,,What have you been doing here?
Dialogue: 0,0:17:46.80,0:17:48.90,Default,,0,0,0,,May it please your Majesty...
Dialogue: 0,0:17:49.50,0:17:52.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Two began, going down on one knee as he spoke.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:53.00,0:17:54.80,Default,,0,0,0,,We were trying—
Dialogue: 0,0:17:55.40,0:17:57.50,Default,,0,0,0,,I see!
Dialogue: 0,0:17:58.10,0:18:01.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen, who had been examining the roses, screamed...
Dialogue: 0,0:18:01.80,0:18:03.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Off with their heads!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:04.40,0:18:07.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The procession moved on, three of the soldiers remaining behind to execute the unfortunate gardeners.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:08.40,0:18:11.20,Default,,0,0,0,,They ran to Alice for protection.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:11.80,0:18:13.90,Default,,0,0,0,,You shan't be beheaded!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:14.50,0:18:17.90,Default,,0,0,0,,She put them into a large flower-pot that stood near.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:18.40,0:18:21.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The three soldiers wandered about for a minute or two, looking for them.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:22.10,0:18:24.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Then they quietly marched off after the others.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:25.10,0:18:27.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Are their heads off?
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.80,0:18:29.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Their heads are gone, if it please your Majesty!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:30.50,0:18:32.20,Default,,0,0,0,,That's right!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:32.80,0:18:34.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Can you play croquet?
Dialogue: 0,0:18:35.40,0:18:37.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The question was evidently meant for Alice.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.80,0:18:39.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Yes!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:40.10,0:18:41.80,Default,,0,0,0,,Come on, then!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:42.40,0:18:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The procession joined in and continued its march.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:46.40,0:18:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice joined the procession, wondering very much what would happen next.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:50.10,0:18:52.90,Default,,0,0,0,,It's—it's a very fine day!
Dialogue: 0,0:18:53.40,0:18:55.90,Default,,0,0,0,,A timid voice spoke at her side.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:56.50,0:18:58.40,Default,,0,0,0,,She was walking by the White Rabbit, who was peeping anxiously into her face.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:00.10,0:19:01.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Very. Where's the Duchess?
Dialogue: 0,0:19:02.50,0:19:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Hush! Hush!
Dialogue: 0,0:19:05.40,0:19:08.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The Rabbit looked nervously behind him, and then tipped up on tiptoe.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:09.10,0:19:12.40,Default,,0,0,0,,He put his mouth close to her ear, and whispered...
Dialogue: 0,0:19:13.00,0:19:15.50,Default,,0,0,0,,She's under sentence of execution.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:16.10,0:19:17.90,Default,,0,0,0,,What for?
Dialogue: 0,0:19:18.40,0:19:20.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Did you say "What a pity!"?
Dialogue: 0,0:19:21.10,0:19:22.80,Default,,0,0,0,,No, I didn't.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:23.40,0:19:25.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I don't think it's at all a pity. I said "What for?"
Dialogue: 0,0:19:26.40,0:19:28.50,Default,,0,0,0,,She boxed the Queen's ears—
Dialogue: 0,0:19:29.10,0:19:32.40,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice gave a little scream of laughter.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:33.00,0:19:35.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Oh, hush!
Dialogue: 0,0:19:35.80,0:19:38.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Rabbit whispered in a frightened tone.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:39.50,0:19:41.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen will hear you! You see, she came rather late...
Dialogue: 0,0:19:42.40,0:19:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And the Queen said— Get to your places!
Dialogue: 0,0:19:46.40,0:19:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The Queen shouted in a voice of thunder, and people began running about in all directions.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:50.10,0:19:52.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Tumbling up against each other.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:53.40,0:19:56.50,Default,,0,0,0,,However, they got settled down in a minute or two.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:57.10,0:19:59.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And the game began immediately.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:01.20,0:20:24.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice thought she had never seen such a curious croquet-ground in all her life.\NEt was all ridges and furrows.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:25.10,0:20:48.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The balls were live hedgehogs, and the mallets live flamingoes.\NThe soldiers had to double themselves up and to stand on their hands and feet, to make the arches.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:49.40,0:21:02.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The chief difficulty Alice found at first was in managing her flamingo.\NShe succeeded in getting its body tucked away, comfortably enough, under her arm.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:03.10,0:21:18.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Its legs were hanging down, but generally, just as she had got its neck nicely straightened out...\NAnd was going to give the hedgehog a blow with its head...
Dialogue: 0,0:21:19.40,0:21:32.80,Default,,0,0,0,,It WOULD twist itself round and look up into her face.\NWith such a puzzled expression that she could not help bursting out laughing.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:33.40,0:21:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And when she had got its head down, and was going to begin again...\NEt was very provoking to find that the hedgehog had unrolled itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:50.10,0:22:04.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And was in the act of crawling away.\NBesides all this, there was generally a ridge or a furrow in the way wherever she wanted to send the hedgehog.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:04.80,0:22:19.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And as the doubled-up soldiers were always getting up and walking to other parts of the ground...\NAlice soon came to the conclusion that it was a very difficult game indeed.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:20.10,0:22:34.80,Default,,0,0,0,,The players all played at once without waiting for turns, quarrelling all the while, and fighting for the hedgehogs.\NThe Queen was in a furious passion, and went stamping about, shouting "Off with his head!" or "Off with her head!" about once in a minute.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:35.40,0:22:42.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice began to feel very uneasy.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:42.80,0:22:58.90,Default,,0,0,0,,To be sure, she had not as yet had any dispute with the Queen.\NBut she knew that it might happen any minute.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:59.40,0:23:12.50,Default,,0,0,0,,*And then, what would become of me? They're dreadfully fond of beheading people here.*\N*The great wonder is, that there's any one left alive!*
Dialogue: 0,0:23:13.10,0:23:24.20,Default,,0,0,0,,She was looking about for some way of escape.\NWondering whether she could get away without being seen...
Dialogue: 0,0:23:24.80,0:23:38.90,Default,,0,0,0,,When she noticed a curious appearance in the air.\NEt puzzled her very much at first, but, after watching it a minute or two, she made it out to be a grin.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:39.40,0:23:42.20,Default,,0,0,0,,It's the Cheshire Cat!
Dialogue: 0,0:23:42.80,0:23:45.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Now I shall have somebody to talk to.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:46.40,0:23:48.50,Default,,0,0,0,,How are you getting on?
Dialogue: 0,0:23:49.10,0:23:51.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The Cat waited until more of it appeared before speaking.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:52.40,0:23:55.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice waited till the eyes appeared, and then nodded.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:55.80,0:23:58.90,Default,,0,0,0,,*It's no use speaking to it, till its ears have come out, or at least one of them.*
Dialogue: 0,0:23:59.40,0:24:12.50,Default,,0,0,0,,In another minute the whole head appeared.\NAlice put down her flamingo, and began an account of the game.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:13.10,0:24:28.20,Default,,0,0,0,,Feeling very glad she had some one to listen to her.\NThe Cat seemed to think that there was enough of it now in sight, and no more of it appeared.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:28.80,0:24:42.90,Default,,0,0,0,,I don't think they play at all fairly.\NAnd they all quarrel so dreadfully one can't hear oneself speak.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:43.40,0:24:58.50,Default,,0,0,0,,And they don't seem to have any rules in particular.\NAt least, if there are, nobody attends to them.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:59.10,0:25:12.20,Default,,0,0,0,,And you've no idea how confusing it is all the things being alive.\NFor instance, there's the arch I've got to go through next walking about at the other end of the ground.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:12.80,0:25:28.90,Default,,0,0,0,,And I should have croqueted the Queen's hedgehog just now.\NBut it ran away when it saw mine coming!
Dialogue: 0,0:25:29.40,0:25:32.20,Default,,0,0,0,,How do you like the Queen?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:32.80,0:25:35.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Not at all. She's so extremely—
Dialogue: 0,0:25:36.40,0:25:39.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice noticed that the King was standing close behind her, listening.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:40.50,0:25:43.40,Default,,0,0,0,,...likely to win, that it's hardly worth while finishing the game.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:44.10,0:25:46.90,Default,,0,0,0,,The King smiled and walked past.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:50.50,0:26:12.20,Default,,0,0,0,,The game drew closer to its wild end.\NArches collapsed and players scattered across the ridges.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:12.80,0:26:34.90,Default,,0,0,0,,Alice gathered her flamingo once more, looking back at the fading grin in the air.\NThe match was over, and the twilight began to fall over the cards.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:35.40,0:27:58.50,Action_Parenthetical,,0,0,0,,(shuffling cards) The procession reformed, marching slowly back toward the palace doors.\NAlice followed behind, ready for whatever trial or feast awaited them inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:01.50,0:28:15.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts, ♪\N♪ All on a summer day; ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:28:15.60,0:28:30.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts, ♪\N♪ And took them clean away! ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:28:32.00,0:28:45.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Will you walk a little faster? ♪\N♪ Said a whiting to a snail. ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:28:45.60,0:28:58.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ There's a porpoise close behind us, ♪\N♪ And he's treading on my tail. ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:28:58.60,0:29:12.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ See how eagerly the lobsters ♪\N♪ And the turtles all advance! ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:29:12.60,0:29:26.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ They are waiting on the shingle— ♪\N♪ Will you come and join the dance? ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:29:26.60,0:29:40.00,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Will you, won't you, will you, ♪\N♪ Won't you, will you join the dance? ♪
Dialogue: 0,0:29:40.60,0:29:58.50,Song,,0,0,0,,♪ Will you, won't you, will you, ♪\N♪ Won't you, won't you join the dance? ♪